diagnosis_text:
Location C’s LAN (192.168.200.0/24) and its router are operating normally. From all four Location C PCs (pc11–pc14), ICMP to the local default gateway 192.168.200.1 (router4) succeeds with 0% loss and sub‑3 ms RTTs (e.g., pc11→192.168.200.1 OK). Those same PCs also reach other sites: pc11–pc14 can ping 172.16.10.5 (remote subnet) with 0% loss. Upstream reachability from Location C is verified as well: pc11 can ping router5 (172.20.1.2) and router3 (10.50.1.1) successfully.

On router4, the inside interface eth0 is UP with 192.168.200.1/24; its uplinks eth4 (192.168.15.2/24) and eth5 (172.25.1.1/24) are UP, and the routing table shows a valid default route via 172.25.1.2 plus RIP-learned routes to the rest of the network. Router5 also has a route back to 192.168.200.0/24 via 192.168.15.2, confirming bidirectional routing.

One endpoint (pc15, 172.18.51.15) does not answer ICMP from Location C (pc11–pc14 time out), but local hosts on its subnet can ping it (pc16/17/18→pc15 OK). This is consistent with a host firewall or policy on that subnet, not a Location C outage.

Root cause: No network fault at Location C; connectivity is presently healthy. The original report likely reflected a transient issue or tests against ICMP-restricted hosts.

Corrective fix: None required on the network. If users still see issues, verify their host DNS/settings and avoid using ICMP to test hosts that block it; collect timestamps and affected destinations for further review. 
=== END ===